@startuml
' Figure 5.7 – System sequence diagram: hybrid search with reciprocal rank fusion
!include _style.puml
mainframe interaction SequenceDiagram Hybrid search
autonumber "0 :"

participant "Visitor" as V
participant "Browser\napplication" as W
participant "System" as A
participant "Embedding\nprovider" as E
participant "Database" as D

V -> W : Type a query
activate W
W -> A : GET /search?q=
activate A

par lexical branch
  A -> D : Rank by ts_rank over the generated tsvector
  activate D
  D --> A : Articles ordered by lexical relevance
  deactivate D
else semantic branch
  A -> E : Embed the query
  activate E
  E --> A : Query vector
  deactivate E
  A -> D : Order by cosine distance, best chunk per article
  activate D
  D --> A : Articles ordered by semantic similarity
  deactivate D
end

A -> A : Fuse the two rankings\nscore = sum of 1 / (60 + rank)
A -> D : Load the merged articles
activate D
D --> A : Article rows
deactivate D
A --> W : Fused results, plus matching writers
deactivate A
W --> V : Render the results
deactivate W

note over V, D
  Fusion is by position, not by score: ts_rank and cosine
  similarity are unrelated scales, so ranks are the only common
  currency. Measured: the query "how do I stop my ferments going
  bad" returns 0 lexical and 2 semantic hits, fused to 2 — no
  article contains the searcher's words.
end note
@enduml
